examples.solution_text:
Use the back bearing at $B$ to find the included angle, then the cosine rule for $AC$.

$\begin{aligned}\angle ABC&=225^{\circ}-120^{\circ}\\&=105^{\circ}\\AC&=\sqrt{40^2+65^2-2(40)(65)\cos105^{\circ}}\\AC&=84.6809\ldots\text{ km}\end{aligned}$

Use the sine rule with the unrounded distance $d=\sqrt{5825-5200\cos105^{\circ}}$.

$\begin{aligned}\frac{\sin\angle ACB}{40}&=\frac{\sin105^{\circ}}{d}\\\angle ACB&=\sin^{-1}\left(\frac{40\sin105^{\circ}}{d}\right)\\&=27.1464\ldots^{\circ}\end{aligned}$

The bearing of $B$ from $C$ is $300^{\circ}$. The direction $CA$ lies anticlockwise from $CB$.

$\begin{aligned}\text{Bearing of }A\text{ from }C&=300^{\circ}-27.1464\ldots^{\circ}\\&\approx273^{\circ}\text{T}\end{aligned}$

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);\coordinate (B) at (1.414,1.414);\coordinate (C) at (4.229,-0.211);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;\draw[->,dashed] (C)--(4.229,1.7) node[above] {$N$};
\pic[draw,"$27.1^{\circ}$",angle radius=13mm,angle eccentricity=1.35] {angle=B--C--A};
\path (A) node[below left] {$A$} (B) node[above] {$B$} (C) node[below right] {$C$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
